[VERDICT — D47a]
  THE INSTRUMENT IS FIT.  The separator is constructed, not cited: arcs shatter 3 and never 4; caps on an exact-rational tetrahedron shatter 4, opposite-edge case included with its certificate.  Both instruments exhibit a true positive AND a true negative, they agree where both apply, and SG1(d) exhibits a system separating them — so shatter-4 may never be read as arc-realizability.
  SG3 WAS VACUOUS AND SAYS SO: an 18-point M^{2+1} record has skies of at most 2 directions against the 4 the test needs, so the original consistency check carried ZERO information.  The pin's pre-registered 'capacity insufficient' expectation was realized at the CONTROL, before any transport data.
  SG10 CERTIFIES THE SCALE REQUIRED, and SG3b then runs the control where it actually decides: 554 decidable pairs across three exact M^{2+1} records, ZERO shattered 4-sets.
  **THE DEMOTION — the sharpest thing this receipt found.**  CIRCULAR-ONES REJECTED 121 OF 554 GENUINE 2+1 SKIES.  A discrete sky of real Minkowski is NOT generally an arc system, so arc-realizability is NOT a usable proxy for 2+1 and Instrument 1 is DEMOTED to a diagnostic.  D47b must rest on SHATTER-4 ALONE.  Any earlier framing of circular-ones as the primary two-sided test is WITHDRAWN: it is two-sided on ARC-REALIZABILITY, which is not the same question.
  ONE-SIDEDNESS RESTATED FOR THE RECORD: nothing in this receipt licenses any statement of the form 'the sky IS a circle'.  Only the obstruction direction is a certificate — and SG3b is the empirical proof of why that restriction was necessary.
